# OnionGate

Tauri 2 desktop app (React UI + Rust core) that routes selected apps through
isolated Tor circuits, hosts temporary and permanent onion sites via Onion Host,
and runs live routing/leak diagnostics. It is **not** a VPN, Tor Browser, Tails,
a two-machine Tor gateway, an antivirus, or a general application firewall.

## Non-negotiable invariants (apply everywhere)

- **Fail closed.** Never silently fall back to a direct route when Tor, TUN, or
  the route policy is unavailable. Guarded apps must suspend rather than leak.
- **Never weaken Tor circuits** or downgrade isolation just to "make it work."
- **Never log, export, upload, or commit secrets.** Private v3 client
  credentials and verification public IPs remain memory-only; full process
  command lines never enter storage or reports. User-selected bridge lines may
  persist only in protected local settings/library storage so they survive a
  restart, never in logs or exports. Managed Tor may write its runtime control
  cookie inside its own protected data directory; OnionGate may read it for
  local authentication but never copy or export it. A **permanent** Onion Host
  site's key is generated and owned by *Tor* inside its own data directory at
  owner-only permissions. OnionGate must never read, copy, log, or export that
  key — it may only read the public `hostname` file. Temporary sites still use
  `DiscardPK` and persist nothing.
- **Every privileged/network mutation** (proxy, TUN, firewall, Tor, transport)
  must be journaled for crash recovery and be reversible.
- **GUI and CLI call the same orchestration APIs.** No command may report a
  protected state until all required components are verified live.
- **Pin every bundled sidecar** by SHA-256 in `scripts/dependencies.sha256`;
  never consume untrusted bridge feeds.

## Where things live

`src/` React UI · `src-tauri/src/` Rust core · `scripts/` dependency fetch ·
`.github/` CI/release · `docs/` VitePress site (`guide/` how-to, `reference/`
architecture, threat model, privacy, release). Root-level docs are the
GitHub-conventional ones only: `README`, `SECURITY`, `CONTRIBUTING`,
`CODE_OF_CONDUCT`.
